.TH MCH 1 "2026-05-31" "meshherder 0.9.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mch \- manage MeshCore repeater nodes through the mchd daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mch
[\fB--socket\fR \fIPATH\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR
[\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mch
is the command line client for Mesh Herder. It talks to
.BR mchd (8)
over a Unix socket and shows fleet status, validates configuration,
triggers syncs, runs carefully checked one-off commands, and guides node
commissioning.
.PP
Mesh Herder treats
.I config.yaml
as desired state and the SQLite database as observed state. The daemon applies
only the settings that are explicitly configured for enforcement; the default
setting policy is audit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the installed version and exit.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-socket \fIPATH\fR
Path to the
.B mchd
Unix socket. Defaults to
.IR /run/meshherder/mchd.sock .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show command help.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show the fleet table, including state, label, group, firmware, neighbour count,
and last seen time.
.TP
.B status \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Show one matching node in detail, or a filtered table if more than one node
matches. The detail view shows facts, settings (desired vs observed, including
settings from config not yet synced), last seen timestamp with relative time,
and recent activity logs.
.TP
.B validate
Validate
.IR /etc/meshherder/config.yaml .
This command does not contact
.BR mchd .
If the secrets file is not readable, the readable config is still checked and
secrets are reported as not checked.
Unrecognised names in the configured facts list are reported as warnings with
close registered-name suggestions where possible; they remain permitted for
new firmware reads that are not yet represented in mch metadata.
.TP
.B validate \-\-config \fIPATH\fR
Validate an explicit config file.
.TP
.B plan
Show desired-versus-known changes without sending mesh commands or writing to
nodes.
.TP
.B plan \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Filter planned writes, audit drift, and unknown values.
.TP
.B set \fINODE KEY\fR [\fIVALUE\fR] [\-\-policy \fIenforce|audit|skip\fR]
Record a desired setting in the database, the authoritative source of desired
state. \fIKEY\fR is a writable setting (for example \fBname\fR or \fBtxdelay\fR) or
a directive (\fBname_prefix\fR, \fBdefault_policy\fR, \fBvia_radio\fR). The write is
synchronous and does not trigger a sync; run
.B sync
.I NODE
to converge. Use
.B \-\-group
.I NAME
or
.B \-\-global
to set the value at group or global scope instead of one node.
.TP
.B unset \fINODE KEY\fR
Remove a database-set desired setting; observed state is left intact. Accepts
.B \-\-group
.I NAME
and
.B \-\-global
like
.BR set .
.TP
.B import [\fIFILE\fR] [\-\-yes]
Seed the database's desired state and node label/group inventory from
.IR config.yaml ,
or from an explicit
.I FILE
(a legacy or exported config), flattening settings and directives at node, group,
and global scope into desired rows. The explicit-file form lets the migration
on-ramp work against a daemon that no longer keeps desired state in its own
.IR config.yaml .
Without
.B \-\-yes
this is a dry-run preview. Re-running is idempotent. A node identified by a full
64-hex public key (as
.B export
emits) is created even if the gateway has not seen it yet \(em the restore path; a
node given only a short prefix needs to be known already and is otherwise skipped
with a warning. If the file contains retired nodes (tagged
.BR "retired: true" ),
choose
.B \-\-retired keep
to restore them as retired or
.B \-\-retired skip
to omit them; a write is refused until one is chosen.
.TP
.B export
Print the whole fleet as config.yaml-shaped YAML on standard output \(em a complete
backup and the inverse of
.BR import .
Includes every node's full public key, label, group, desired settings, and
operations (even a node with no desired settings); retired nodes are tagged
.BR "retired: true" .
Redirect to save, for example
.BR "mch export > backup.yaml" ,
and rebuild after a server or gateway loss with
.BR "mch import backup.yaml \-\-yes" .
.TP
.B suggest
Show suggested settings for all nodes based on neighbour count.
.TP
.B suggest \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Show detailed suggestions and a paste-ready config block for one node.
.TP
.B logs
Show recent per-node activity across all nodes.
.TP
.B logs \fIPREFIX\fR
Show recent activity for one node.
.TP
.B contacts
Show repeater contacts visible to the gateway that are not yet in config.
.TP
.B retire \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Stop managing a node: set its state to
.BR retired ,
unfavourite its gateway contact (clearing the eviction pin; the contact itself is
kept), and exclude it from sync, while keeping its history. Retire-don't-delete is
the normal way to decommission a node.
.TP
.B unretire \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Return a retired node to management: re-favourite it and queue it to reconverge.
.TP
.B prune
List retired nodes \(em the candidates for a hard delete.
.TP
.B prune \-\-yes
Hard-delete retired nodes' local database state (facts, settings, operations, logs,
and the node row). This does not edit
.IR secrets.yaml .
.TP
.B clean facts \fINODE\fR [\-\-fact \fIKEY\fR] [\-\-yes]
List or delete cached fact rows that are no longer present in the configured
.B facts:
list. Scope may be one node,
.B \-\-group
.IR NAME ,
or
.B \-\-all .
Without
.B \-\-yes
this is a dry run.
.TP
.B clean settings \fINODE\fR [\-\-setting \fIKEY\fR] [\-\-yes]
List or delete observed setting rows whose key is no longer in the node's resolved
desired set (config plus database). Scope may be one node,
.B \-\-group
.IR NAME ,
or
.B \-\-all .
Without
.B \-\-yes
this is a dry run. Observed rows are a cache and are re-read on the next sync if
still wanted.
.TP
.B remove \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Delete one node's local database state after confirmation. This deletes local
facts, settings, operations, logs, and the database node row. It refuses a
still-managed (non-retired) node unless
.B \-\-force
is supplied, because an un-retired node is re-seeded from gateway favourites on the
next restart \(em prefer
.B retire
for active nodes.
.TP
.B remove \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-yes
Delete without prompting.
.TP
.B sync
Start an immediate full sync cycle in the background. Rate limiting still
applies.
.TP
.B sync \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Sync one node immediately while streaming fact reads, setting checks, enforcement
actions, operations, and timeout failures as they occur.
.TP
.B path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Show the gateway's cached management route metadata for one node without probing
the mesh. Routes may be
.BR direct ,
.BR stored ,
.BR flood ,
or
.BR missing .
.B flood
and
.B missing
are marked
.B "path repair needed"
because full management syncs avoid flood-routed contacts.
.TP
.B path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-discover
Ask MeshCore for a fresh path-discovery candidate and print the value to review.
The gateway contact is not changed.
.TP
.B path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-advert
Print the gateway's cached advert path candidate without changing the contact.
.TP
.B path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-apply \fIPATH[:MODE]\fR
Apply an operator-approved replacement path to the gateway contact. Re-run
.B sync
for the node after applying. A wrong path can make management less reachable
until repaired again.
.TP
.B discover \-\-radio \fIFREQ,BW,SF,CR\fR [\-\-dwell \fISECONDS\fR] [\-\-unknown\-only]
Temporarily switch the gateway radio, send an active MeshCore discovery request,
collect responses for a bounded dwell period, then restore the gateway radio.
This prints discovered public keys, type, SNR, status, and matching label. It
does not add DB nodes, gateway contacts, secrets, or config entries. With
.B \-\-unknown\-only ,
fully managed nodes are hidden while config-only, DB-only, and unmanaged nodes
remain visible.
.TP
.B adopt \fIPUBKEY\fR \-\-radio \fIFREQ,BW,SF,CR\fR [\-\-label \fILABEL\fR]
Adopt an already-running discovered repeater by full 64-character public key.
This adds/updates the local DB node, adds and favourites a gateway contact,
installs a direct (zero-hop) route on it, then sends one directed
.B ver
on the discovered radio to confirm the route carries a command (restoring the
gateway radio afterwards). It does not edit
.IR config.yaml ,
.IR secrets.yaml ,
or hardware settings.
.TP
.B probe \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-radio \fIFREQ,BW,SF,CR\fR [\-\-login] [\-\-advert]
Read-only reachability check: temporarily switch the gateway radio, send one
directed
.B ver
to the node, restore the radio, and report the result.
.B \-\-login
authenticates with the node's configured password first (an auth-required node
silently drops unauthenticated commands, so a bare
.B ver
times out).
.B \-\-advert
sends a gateway zero-hop advert on the radio first so the node can learn a route
back. Changes nothing on the node, the contact route,
.IR config.yaml ,
or
.IR secrets.yaml .
.TP
.B exec \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \fICOMMAND...\fR
Run a raw MeshCore CLI command on a managed node. Dangerous commands are blocked
or require confirmation.
.TP
.B new
Interactively add a fresh or replacement MeshCore node.
.TP
.B start
Start
.B mchd
through systemd.
.TP
.B stop
Stop
.B mchd
through systemd.
.TP
.B restart
Restart
.B mchd
through systemd.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/meshherder/config.yaml
Desired fleet state.
.TP
.I /etc/meshherder/secrets.yaml
Node passwords and private keys. Usually mode 600 and readable only by the
service account.
.TP
.I /var/lib/meshherder/mch.db
Observed state, command history, facts, and sync schedule.
.TP
.I /run/meshherder/mchd.sock
Default daemon socket.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B mch validate
Validate the active config file, and validate secrets too if they are readable.
.TP
.B sudo mch validate
Validate both production config and mode-600 secrets.
.TP
.B mch status qh
Show nodes matching
.IR qh .
.TP
.B mch sync qh-wold
Sync one node immediately and show live progress while waiting for mesh replies.
.TP
.B mch remove old-site --yes
Delete local DB state for a retired node.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mchd (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
